// ═══ WORK-STEALING MECHANISM ═══
// Worker-1 deque: [A][B][C][D]  ← Worker-1 pops from FRONT (D first — LIFO!)
// Worker-2 deque: []             ← empty!
// Worker-2 STEALS from Worker-1's BACK:
// Worker-1 deque: [A][B][C]  (D being processed by W1, A stolen by W2!)
// Worker-2 deque: [A]         (processing A!)

// WHY LIFO for owner? Cache locality — most recently forked task
//   likely shares data with parent → hot in CPU cache!
// WHY FIFO for thief? Oldest task = largest subtree = more work to steal!
//   Stealing one big task = many small tasks to work on!

// ═══ COMMON POOL DANGER ═══
ForkJoinPool common = ForkJoinPool.commonPool();
// parallelism: Runtime.availableProcessors() - 1 (e.g., 3 on 4-core)
// Shared by: parallelStream(), supplyAsync(), framework internals

// DANGEROUS: blocking IO on common pool!
IntStream.range(0, 100).parallel().forEach(i -> {
    httpCall(url);  // blocks 200ms! All 3 workers stuck!
    // parallelStream in ANOTHER part of the app → STALLED!
    // CompletableFuture.supplyAsync() → STALLED!
});

// FIX: dedicated pool for IO!
ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(50);
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> httpCall(url), ioPool);  // NOT common pool!
}
// Common pool: free for CPU-bound parallelStream!

// ═══ COMPENSATING WORKER THREADS ═══
// ForkJoinPool has a compensation mechanism:
// If a worker blocks (ManagedBlocker), pool creates a TEMPORARY worker
// to maintain parallelism! But: limited, adds overhead, not reliable for IO!
ForkJoinPool.managedBlock(new ForkJoinPool.ManagedBlocker() {
    public boolean block() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(1000);  // blocking operation
        return true;
    }
    public boolean isReleasable() { return false; }
});
// Pool may create a temporary thread to compensate for the blocked one.
// But: not a solution for bulk IO! Use dedicated pools!
```

